Sept. 14, 5:40 p.m., County of Kaua=CA=BBi, Hurricane Lowell Recovery Update

L=C4=AAHU=CA=BBE =E2=80=93 A week after Hurricane Lowell caused wreckage ac=
ross Kaua=E2=80=99i, communities are moving into the next phase of recovery.

The County is preparing to open two disaster recovery resource centers for =
storm victims to provide vital information on how to rebuild. Meanwhile, as=
 critical infrastructure is restored, the County will be closing two more o=
f its Hurricane Refuge Areas.

The county has also launched a new website where storm victims can find for=
ms necessary to submit damage assessments and file for disaster tax relief.

New assessment numbers show 10 homes have been destroyed and 64 suffered ma=
jor damage.

The total number of resident-submitted damage assessments has surpassed 620.

For recovery help, resources and updates, visit www.kauairecovers.org [ htt=
ps://www.kauairecovers.org/?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ].

For any non-emergency information, call Aloha United Way at 211.

=C2=A0

*Evening update - at a glance*


  * Tropical Storm Norbert weakening with no severe impacts expected at thi=
s time=20
  * Disaster recovery resource centers opening tomorrow=20
  * Two Hurricane Refuge Areas closing Wednesday=20
  * K=C4=ABlauea and Hanalei Elementary schools prepare to reopen=20
  * New website launched to submit damage assessments and file for tax reli=
ef=20
  * Traffic delays continue as transmission line repairs push forward on th=
e North Shore=20
  * Regular bus and paratransit service resumed today=20
  * Normal weekday islandwide refuse collection resumes in accessible areas=
=20
  * Power is restored for 81% of the island=20
  * Island-wide water conservation notice remains in effect=20

=C2=A0

*Resource Centers Opening Tomorrow (NEW UPDATE)*

The County is set to open two disaster recovery resource centers tomorrow, =
Sept. 15; one on the North Shore at the Hanalei Neighborhood Center, the ot=
her on the south shore at the K=C5=8Dloa Neighborhood Center.=C2=A0


Both centers will operate from 9 a.m. to 6 p.m. Residents can meet with non=
profit and agency staff in-person for assistance with recovery needs.


The Hanalei initiative will operate an hourly shuttle starting 9 a.m. from =
The Hanalei Colony resort in Wainiha to the Hanalei Resource Center.


=C2=A0

*Refuge areas closing (NEW UPDATE)*

With power restoration efforts progressing across Kaua=E2=80=98i, one Refug=
e Area will remain open while two others will close on Wednesday, Sept. 16,=
 at 10 a.m.


  * Closing: Kal=C4=81heo and L=C4=ABhu=CA=BBe neighborhood centers=20
  * Remaining open: K=C4=ABlauea Neighborhood Center, 2460 Keneke St.=20


Individuals utilizing refuge areas have the opportunity to connect with cas=
e management and other wraparound services and support.=C2=A0

=C2=A0

*More support incoming for power restoration (NEW UPDATE)*

Nearly 200 utility workers from Kaua=CA=BBi, O=CA=BBahu, Big Island, Maui a=
nd the mainland are deployed across the island enhancing restoration effort=
s, and more are on the way.

With KIUC receiving support from Hawaiian Electric, Southern California Edi=
son, and HMS, this week about 250 workers are expected to be on assignment =
focused on restoring electricity.

Teams have isolated a section of a transmission line from Princeville to Wa=
iniha, allowing crews to safely complete repairs in the affected area while=
 simultaneously reenergizing surrounding areas where power can be safely re=
stored.

This work is part of restoration work prompting a lane closure on the Hanal=
ei side of the Hanalei Bridge.

No timeline is available on when repairs will be complete or when the lane =
closure will be lifted. Drivers should be prepared for extended delays.

Flaggers will be on site for traffic control, but please use extra caution =
around coned work areas.

We appreciate everyone=E2=80=99s patience, support, and cooperation while t=
his important work is completed. Please slow down, stay alert, and be safe.=
=C2=A0

Power is now restored for about 81% of the island as of Monday afternoon. R=
oughly 6.7K members are still without power. Peak outages impacted about 33=
,000 customers. KIUC has 13 of 16 transmission lines back up.

Stay away from downed utility lines and call 911 if a line is an immediate =
danger. Update your contact information with KIUC at 808-246-4300 to get al=
erts. Outage map: kiuc.outagemap.coop [ https://kiuc.outagemap.coop/?utm_me=
dium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery#/ ]

=C2=A0

*Most schools reopen across the island (NEW UPDATE)*

Most Kaua=CA=BBi public schools reopened Monday, Sept. 14; K=C4=ABlauea and=
 Hanalei elementary schools remained closed amid power restoration but now =
have set reopening dates and contingency plans to operate without electrici=
ty.

Reopening schedule:


  * Tuesday, Sept. 15: K=C4=ABlauea Elementary reopening to students and st=
aff=20
  * Friday, Sept. 18 Hanalei Elementary reopening to employees only=20
  * Monday, Sept. 21: Hanalei Elementary reopening to students=20

While Hanalei Elementary remains closed, the school is working to provide i=
nstructional packets and shelf-stable meal boxes for families to pick up th=
is week. Details will be provided as information becomes available.


(Chiefess Kamakahelei Middle School reopening Sept. 15 after pre-Lowell sch=
eduled closure on Monday, Sept. 14)=C2=A0

All Kaua=CA=BBi public schools will provide nonperishable, shelf-stable mea=
ls.

Transportation: Special education curb-to-curb service will continue. Some =
regular education bus routes remain unavailable or may operate with modifie=
d service due to road conditions and detours. Affected families have been c=
ontacted by schools.

Information: Hawai=CA=BBi Department of Education school and transportation=
 updates at https://hawaiipublicschools.org/2026-kauai-reopen-hurricane-low=
ell/ [ https://hawaiipublicschools.org/2026-kauai-reopen-hurricane-lowell/?=
utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ] andhttps://hawaiipublicschool=
s.org/2026-kauai-school-bus-route-information/ [ https://hawaiipublicschool=
s.org/2026-kauai-school-bus-route-information/?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_sourc=
e=3Dgovdelivery ].

=C2=A0

*Tax relief and damage assessments (NEW UPDATE)*

To help make it easier for storm victims to submit damage assessments and f=
ile for disaster tax relief, the County has launched a new website with lin=
ks to necessary forms and documents.

Visit kauai.gov/lowellrealproperty [ http://kauai.gov/lowellrealproperty?ut=
m_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ] if your home, business, farm, o=
r ranch suffered damage from Hurricane Lowell.

To submit a tax remission form:


  * Email: rpassessment@kauai.gov=20

  * Mail: L=C4=ABhu=CA=BBe Civic Center, 4444 Rice Street, Suite 454, L=C4=
=ABhu=CA=BBe, HI 96766=20
  * In-person drop box: L=C4=ABhu=CA=BBe Civic Center Mo=E2=80=98ikeha Rotu=
nda=C2=A0=20

The deadline to submit for a tax remission is June 30, 2027.

=C2=A0

*Mold warning*

After severe flooding, health officials are urging residents to address any=
 mold growth in their homes and on their personal items.

Exposure can elicit serious health risks including breathing problems, alle=
rgic reactions, and infections.=C2=A0

If you plan to clean any areas growing mold, remember the following tips:


  * Wear protective gear such as gloves, N-95 masks, boots and eye protecti=
on during cleanup.=20
  * Before cleaning, lightly spray moldy surfaces with a soapy solution to =
reduce airborne particles.=20
  * Clean with soap and water, then disinfect using a bleach solution (1 cu=
p bleach to 1 gallon of water). Open windows and doors first and air out ro=
oms after cleaning. Never mix bleach with other cleaning products containin=
g ammonia. Use as directed on the label.=20
  * Allow at least 15 minutes of contact time, then rinse and dry thoroughl=
y.=20
  * Discard materials that cannot be safely cleaned, including carpets, mat=
tresses, upholstery and other porous items wet for more than 24=E2=80=9348 =
hours, or items that cannot be laundered or disinfected.=C2=A0=20

=C2=A0

*Household waste and recycling (NEW UPDATE)*

Normal weekday islandwide refuse collection will continue throughout the we=
ek to accessible areas. Residents should only place their carts out for col=
lection on their designated pickup day.

The County is now operating seven sites to dispose of storm debris and refu=
se.

Please check which materials are accepted at your intended disposal site be=
fore making the trip. Each site will only accept specific materials, so the=
 public is advised to appropriately separate their waste items before makin=
g any drop-offs.

Site hours, locations, and accepted materials are as follows:


  * Hanalei Transfer Station | 7:15 a.m. - 3:15 p.m. | K=C5=ABhi=C5=8D High=
way | Debris=20
  * K=C4=ABlauea Temporary Site | 7:15 a.m. - 3:15 p.m. | K=C5=ABhi=C5=8D H=
ighway | Green Waste (located past the K=C4=ABlauea roundabout and across t=
he street from Anaina Hou Community Park)=20
  * Kapa=CA=BBa Temporary Site | 7:15 a.m. - 3:15 p.m. | Kapa=CA=BBa Bypass=
 Road | Green Waste (accessed via the southbound (one-way) lane of Kapa=CA=
=BBa Bypass Road adjacent to theBryan J. Baptiste Sports Complex)=20
  * Kapa=CA=BBa Transfer Station | 7:15 a.m. - 3:15 p.m. | Kahau Road | Deb=
ris=20
  * L=C4=ABhu=CA=BBe Transfer Station | 7:15 a.m. - 3:15 p.m. | Ahukini Roa=
d | Debris & Green Waste=C2=A0=20
  * Hanap=C4=93p=C4=93 Transfer Station | 8:00 a.m. - 3:15 p.m. | Lele Road=
 | Debris & Green Waste=20
  * Kekaha Landfill | Residential: 8 a.m. - 4 p.m./Commercial 8 a.m. - 3:30=
 p.m. | Kaumuali'i Highway - Debris & Green Waste=20

The public is reminded that all debris (not including green waste) must not=
 be longer than 3 feet.

HI-5 redemption in L=C4=ABhu=CA=BBe has reopened at the Kaua=CA=BBi Resourc=
e Center and is open Monday through Saturday from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. (closed =
from Noon to 1:30 p.m.). Other locations will remain closed until equipment=
 is repaired.

=C2=A0

*The Kaua=CA=BBi Bus*

Regular bus and paratransit service resumed today and will continue through=
 the week.

The 900 and 950 Anahola-Waimea express routes will not run this week.=C2=A0

For bus route information, call 808-246-8110 or visit www.kauai.gov/transpo=
rtation [ https://www.kauai.gov/Government/Departments-Agencies/Transportat=
ion?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ].

=C2=A0

*Neighborhood Centers closed*

All Neighborhood Centers are closed until further notice. The Department of=
 Parks and Recreation is temporarily suspending recreational use of these f=
acilities to support the County=E2=80=99s hurricane response and recovery e=
fforts.=C2=A0

=C2=A0

*Food distribution:*

Food distributions will continue at the following locations:


  * Tuesday, Sept. 15 - Anahola Clubhouse, 3900 Kawelo Street, Anahola | 4 =
to 6 p.m.=20
  * Tuesday, Sept. 15 - K=C5=8Dloa Ball Park, Maluhia Road, K=C5=8Dloa | 3 =
to 5 p.m.=C2=A0=20
  * Thursday, Sept. 17 - St. Raphael=E2=80=99s Church, 3011 Hapa Road, K=C5=
=8Dloa | 4:30 to 6 pm=C2=A0=20
  * Thursday, Sept. 17 - Westside Christian Center, 8041 Kekaha Road, Kekah=
a | 10 a.m. - 12 p.m.=20

=C2=A0

*County closures:*


  * All County parks, campgrounds, and the Wailua Golf Course are closed.=20
  * The Waimea and Kapa=CA=BBa pools are closed.=20
  * County board and commission meetings are suspended.=C2=A0=20
  * The Sept. 16 County Council meeting is canceled.=20
  * All in-person and counter services at the L=C4=ABhu=CA=BBe Civic Center=
 are closed through Friday, Sept. 18 following a fire at the K=C4=81pule Bu=
ilding. Impacted services include Real Property, Planning, building and zon=
ing, Wastewater, sewer billing, and the DMV, including driver=E2=80=99s lic=
enses and vehicle registration.=20

=C2=A0

*State road closures (NEW UPDATE)*=C2=A0


  * Kaumuali=CA=BBi Highway (Vicinity of Halfway Bridge): Tree/debris remov=
al ongoing; crews will use the shoulder to minimize traffic impacts.=C2=A0 =

  * K=C5=ABhi=C5=8D Highway (Vicinity of Princeville Transfer Station to Ka=
paka Street): Tree/Debris removal and one lane shifting. (Tomorrow =E2=80=
=93 8:30 a.m. - 3:00 p.m.).=20
  * K=C5=ABhi=C5=8D Highway (Vicinity of Kalihiwai Bridge): Tree/Debris rem=
oval ongoing and one lane shifting. (Tomorrow =E2=80=93 8:30 a.m. - 3:00 p.=
m.).=20
  * Wainiha Bridge #3 (Double bridge on H=C4=81=CA=BBena Side): Repair work=
 ongoing. Bridge open to local traffic only. 5-ton weight limit.=20
  * Crews will resume debris clearing under the mauka Wailua River Bridge S=
ept. 20.=20
  * K=C5=8Dke=CA=BBe and Waimea Canyon roads are still being cleared.=20

  * Halewili Road is open from the =CA=BBEle=CA=BBele side of Kaumuali=CA=
=BBi Highway but remains closed from the Kal=C4=81heo side of the highway t=
o Kaua=CA=BBi Coffee due to downed utility poles.=20
  * K=C5=ABhi=C5=8D Highway: Princeville to Ke=CA=BBe Beach open to local t=
raffic only.=20

=C2=A0

*Airport and harbors:*


  * L=C4=ABhu=CA=BBe Airport is open and fully operational. Arrive 90 minut=
es early for interisland flights and two hours early for flights to the con=
tinent.=20
  * N=C4=81wiliwili Harbor is open to cargo ships. No cruise ships are comi=
ng in.=20
  * Port Allen is open for operations.=C2=A0=20

=C2=A0

*Other closures and advisories:*


  * State parks and forest reserves are closed.=20
  * The Pacific Missile Range Facility has closed its recreation sites and =
guest pass program.=20
  * A brown water advisory remains in effect islandwide. Stay out of floodw=
ater and out of the ocean near stream mouths, drains and harbors. The water=
 may carry sewage, animal waste, chemicals and debris.=20

More closures are possible as crews continue clearing roads.

=C2=A0

*Department of Water*

An island-wide water conservation notice remains in effect and will be lift=
ed when KIUC is able to fully restore power islandwide. A timeline is unava=
ilable at this time.

Please only use water for essential needs.


  * Outages can affect pumps and wells, so water may cut out or lose pressu=
re even if your home is fine.=20
  * Do not use water if you believe it has been contaminated by flooding.=20

  * To report a water service outage, or leak, call 808-245-5444. Updates a=
re posted at kauaiwater.org [ https://www.kauaiwater.org/?utm_medium=3Demai=
l&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ].=20

=C2=A0

*Post-storm scam warning*

The Kaua=CA=BBi Police Department is reminding residents to remain vigilant=
 for scams during ongoing recovery from Hurricane Lowell. Be wary of unsoli=
cited calls, texts, emails, social media messages, or other communications =
offering assistance or requesting information.=C2=A0


  * For verified information regarding Hurricane Lowell recovery, visit the=
 County of Kaua=CA=BBi=E2=80=99s official channels and KauaiRecovers.org. [=
 https://www.kauairecovers.org/?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery=
 ]=20

=C2=A0

*Driving*

Police are urging the public to travel only if necessary. Officers are esco=
rting work crews, and extra traffic is slowing debris removal, power restor=
ation, and road repairs.


  * Skip non-essential travel. This includes general sightseeing and drivin=
g to view storm damage.=20
  * Pull to the right and stop when an emergency vehicle approaches.=20
  * Never follow a police escort or use one to get into a closed area.=20

  * Obey closures, barricades, and directions from officers and flaggers.=20
  * Treat every dark traffic light as a four-way stop. Lights are out islan=
dwide.=20
  * Report hazards like downed lines or blocked roads. Do not stop to take =
photos.=20

=C2=A0

*Next storm*

State officials are monitoring Tropical Storm Norbert, which is weakening a=
nd is not expected to bring severe impacts to the Hawaiian Islands at this =
time. Residents should continue to monitor official forecasts and take the =
opportunity to review their emergency supplies. For preparedness informatio=
n, visit ready.hawaii.gov [ https://dod.hawaii.gov/hiema/?utm_medium=3Demai=
l&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ].

=C2=A0

*Visitors*

Hawai=CA=BBi is still welcoming visitors but is also urging anyone with upc=
oming trips to the islands to monitor conditions. The state is also encoura=
ging travelers with upcoming trips to Kaua=CA=BBi to consider visiting anot=
her island to allow Kaua=CA=BBi time to heal and recover from Hurricane Low=
ell.

=C2=A0

*Numbers and resources*


  * Kaua=CA=BBi Emergency Management Agency =E2=80=93 kauai.gov/KEMA [ http=
s://www.kauai.gov/Government/Departments-Agencies/Emergency-Management-Agen=
cy?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ]=20
  * National Weather Service =E2=80=93 weather.gov/hfo [ https://www.weathe=
r.gov/hfo?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ] or 808-245-6001=20
  * Department of Water =E2=80=93 808-245-5444 or kauaiwater.org [ https://=
www.kauaiwater.org/?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ]=20
  * Aloha United Way 211 =E2=80=93 808-275-2000 or AUW211.org=20

Use 911 only for life-threatening emergencies. Calls about weather or road =
conditions tie up the line and delay help for others. For non-emergencies, =
call KPD Dispatch at 808-755-8516 or 808-652-7837. KPD=E2=80=99s usual non-=
emergency line, 808-241-1711, is still down.

To sign up for Everbridge, the County=E2=80=99s emergency notification serv=
ice, please visit www.kauai.gov/KEMA [ https://www.kauai.gov/Government/Dep=
artments-Agencies/Emergency-Management-Agency?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=
=3Dgovdelivery ] and click on =E2=80=9CNotification Services.=E2=80=9D You =
may also text =E2=80=9CKAUAIEMA=E2=80=9D to 888777, or call the Kaua=CA=BBi=
 Emergency Management Agency=E2=80=99s alternate numbers at 808-726-0702 or=
 808-347-9833. The KEMA non-emergency line, 808-241-1800, remains unavailab=
le due to ongoing outages.

For recovery help, resources, and updates, visit www.kauairecovers.org [ ht=
tps://www.kauairecovers.org/?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ].

=C2=A0

*Report your damage*

Take photos, write down what was damaged, and file a report at bit.ly/kauai=
damage [ https://survey123.arcgis.com/share/54d0b90fb9a74a1094e89d9f26b5d75=
9?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ]. These reports show how muc=
h of the island was hit and may help Kaua=CA=BBi qualify for state and fede=
ral aid. The state has asked for a fast-tracked presidential disaster decla=
ration. You still need to file a claim with your insurance company.

Report roadway and public space hazards at bit.ly/kauaihazard [ https://sur=
vey123.arcgis.com/share/060659e0fd7344d59ccb2056dcbe53f9?utm_medium=3Demail=
&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ]. Businesses can report damage at bit.ly/lowelld=
amage [ https://survey123.arcgis.com/share/f828eb79bd884e6bbc54535007ba9bf9=
?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dgovdelivery ].

